# A use-after-free: a constructor that pushes onto a List held in one of its own reference fields puts the List's
# chunk in the constructor's own scope, which closes when the constructor returns - the next allocation of the
# caller's then writes over the List. Found building settling agents (agent.olang): the agent's constructor recorded
# its circuit's regions (Push onto the circuit's List of regions) and the agent crashed in a later moment, once a
# loop body allocated text (Fixed). Pushing after construction (h.items.Push(6) in main) is sound, and so is building
# the List in a function called from the field's initializer (fn made() Inner& { ... }) - agent.olang does that.
# Expected: "after a new array: 6" three times. Actual (olang ef939ae): a segmentation fault after the first line, or
# a wrong value where the freed chunk is reused by something else.
# Status: still reproduces on olang 9621af3 - the same segmentation fault after the first line.
import "std/io"

type Holder struct() {
    items mut List<I64>& = List<I64>()
    items.Push(6)
}

fn main() ? io.IoError {
    h := Holder()
    try io.Print("after construction: " $(h.items[0]) "\n")
    for t in range 3 {
        junk := Array<I64>(64, -1)
        try io.Print("after a new array: " $(h.items[0]) "\n")
    }
}
